---
name: idea-brainstormer
description: Brainstorm 5 GenK-style story ideas from a VinFast pipeline article. Invoked as /idea-brainstormer [ID | "search keyword" | empty for picker]. Reads + writes via the VPS HTTP API; SKILLS/.env supplies the bearer token.
---

# /idea-brainstormer — Brainstorm Skill (v2 — HTTP-based)

## Working directory
The VinFast news pipeline project folder (already granted Cowork access).

## Auth (mandatory — per PLAN-v2 §5.3 Auth)

Every bash invocation that touches the API MUST source `SKILLS/.env` first.
No hardcoded tokens. No interactive prompts. Failure to source is a hard exit.

```bash
set -a && . SKILLS/.env && set +a || {
  echo "[error] SKILLS/.env missing or unreadable" >&2
  exit 1
}
```

After sourcing, `$API_BASE_URL` and `$API_TOKEN` are in env. Never echo them.

`api` helper — keep `$API_TOKEN` out of argv via `curl -K -` (config from
stdin). **Define + auth-source at the top of EVERY bash block** (Cowork
sessions don't share env or shell functions across blocks):

```bash
set -a && . SKILLS/.env && set +a || {
  echo "[error] SKILLS/.env missing or unreadable" >&2
  exit 1
}

# api METHOD PATH [BODY_FILE] — prints one line: STATUS<TAB>RETRY_AFTER<TAB>BODY
# 3rd arg is a FILE PATH (never a shell string). Cowork should use its
# Write tool to materialize the JSON payload (which handles all character
# encoding) then call api() with that path. This avoids shell-quoting
# hazards from model-generated content (quotes, backslashes, newlines).
# Does NOT use `curl -fsS` (which would swallow 4xx/5xx body and prevent
# branching on 400/404/429). Caller parses STATUS and BODY explicitly.
api() {
  local method="$1" path="$2" body_file="${3:-}"
  local hdrf bodyf rc
  hdrf=$(mktemp /tmp/.tlinh-h.XXXXXX); bodyf=$(mktemp /tmp/.tlinh-b.XXXXXX)
  local cfg
  cfg=$(printf 'url = "%s%s"\nheader = "Authorization: Bearer %s"\nheader = "Accept: application/json"\n' \
    "$API_BASE_URL" "$path" "$API_TOKEN")
  if [ -n "$body_file" ]; then
    if [ ! -f "$body_file" ]; then
      rm -f "$hdrf" "$bodyf"
      printf '0\t\t{"error":"local","detail":"missing body file %s"}\n' "$body_file"
      return 1
    fi
    cfg+=$'\n''header = "Content-Type: application/json"'
    rc=$(printf '%s' "$cfg" | curl -sS -X "$method" --max-time 60 -K - \
        -D "$hdrf" -o "$bodyf" -w '%{http_code}' --data-binary "@$body_file")
  else
    rc=$(printf '%s' "$cfg" | curl -sS -X "$method" --max-time 60 -K - \
        -D "$hdrf" -o "$bodyf" -w '%{http_code}')
  fi
  local retry_after; retry_after=$(awk 'BEGIN{IGNORECASE=1} /^Retry-After:/ {sub(/^[^:]*:[ \t]*/,""); sub(/\r?$/,""); print; exit}' "$hdrf")
  local out; out=$(cat "$bodyf")
  rm -f "$hdrf" "$bodyf"
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "${rc:-0}" "${retry_after:-}" "$out"
}
```

Below, every `<PREAMBLE>` placeholder means: paste the two blocks above
(auth-source + api() definition) literally at the top of that bash block.

## How users invoke me

| User input | What I do |
|---|---|
| `/idea-brainstormer 42` | Brainstorm article id 42 directly |
| `/idea-brainstormer` (no args) | Show today's top candidates, ask user to pick |
| `/idea-brainstormer "VF8"` | Search title/content for "VF8", show matches |
| `/idea-brainstormer score=5` | Show score=5 candidates not yet brainstormed |
| `/idea-brainstormer yesterday` | Show yesterday's candidates |

## Step-by-step

### Step 1 — Resolve user input to an API call

Inspect the user's argument and build one of these bash blocks. **Each block
includes the `<PREAMBLE>` placeholder — paste the auth-source + api()
definition from the top of this file literally there**, then run.

- **Pure integer** (e.g. `42`) → fetch directly:
  ```bash
  <PREAMBLE>
  RESP=$(api GET '/api/articles/42')
  STATUS=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f1)
  BODY=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f3-)
  echo "status=$STATUS"; echo "$BODY"
  ```
  Server returns a single article object on 200. 404 means no such id.
- **No arg or empty** → today's top candidates not yet brainstormed:
  ```bash
  <PREAMBLE>
  api GET '/api/articles?stage=scored&min_score=3&not_brainstormed=1&last_hours=24'
  ```
  If `rows[]` is empty, relax to `min_score=2`. If still empty, try
  `&last_hours=48`. If still empty, ask user
  "Không có article nào hợp lệ hôm nay. Bạn muốn brainstorm article id nào cụ thể?"
- **Keyword in quotes** (e.g. `"VF8"`) → search (URL-encode spaces as `%20`):
  ```bash
  <PREAMBLE>
  api GET '/api/articles?search=VF8&not_brainstormed=1'
  ```
- **`score=N`** → exact score, not yet brainstormed:
  ```bash
  <PREAMBLE>
  api GET '/api/articles?score=N&not_brainstormed=1'
  ```
- **`yesterday`** → 24–48h window:
  ```bash
  <PREAMBLE>
  api GET '/api/articles?last_hours=48&not_brainstormed=1'
  ```
- **Other free text** → treat as search keyword (strip quotes if any).

In every case: STATUS=200 is the only success status. 429 → wait
`Retry-After` (typically 60s) and retry **exactly ONCE** (this is a
non-lock endpoint, per PLAN-v2 §7.4: `/api/articles/*` gets one retry only,
not three). Anything else → tell the user "API trả về lỗi STATUS" and stop.

### Step 2 — Pick the target id

If Step 1 returned exactly one row → that is the target id; go to Step 3.

If multiple rows (`rows[]` length ≥ 2) → display a compact table:

```
1. [#42] (score 5) VnExpress — VinFast Q1 2026 vượt expect...
2. [#38] (score 4) TuoiTre   — VF8 sắp ra mắt thị trường Mỹ...
3. [#51] (score 4) Electrek  — VinFast partners with US battery...
```

Ask: "Brainstorm article nào? (paste id, ví dụ 42)"
Wait for user reply. Re-parse as integer; if invalid, ask again.

If zero rows → tell user "Không có article phù hợp" and stop.

### Step 3 — Read full article content

```bash
<PREAMBLE>
RESP=$(api GET '/api/articles/<CHOSEN_ID>')
STATUS=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f1)
BODY=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f3-)
[ "$STATUS" = "200" ] || { echo "[fatal] $STATUS: $BODY" >&2; exit 1; }
echo "$BODY"
```

The JSON has `id`, `title`, `source`, `content`, `score`, `score_reason`,
optionally previous `ideas` (decoded JSON, not string).

### Step 4 — Read the guidelines

Read `brainstorm-guidelines.md` from the project root. It defines the output
shape and editorial rules.

### Step 5 — Generate 5 ideas

Apply `brainstorm-guidelines.md` to produce a JSON array of EXACTLY 5 idea
objects, each with fields `title`, `angle`, `format`, `difficulty`,
`viral_potential` (integer 1–5).

Construct the JSON literally as compact one-line. **Validate before Step 6**:
- exactly 5 elements,
- each element has all 5 required fields,
- `viral_potential` is integer 1–5,
- string fields are non-empty.

You may use WebSearch + MCP tools for additional research context before
finalizing the ideas — Cowork has those tools available.

### Step 6 — Write to VPS via PATCH

**Do NOT pass `<JSON_ARRAY_LITERAL>` through a bash command line.** Cowork
uses the **Write tool** (not bash) to materialize the wrapper JSON to a
file path, then api() reads the file via `curl --data-binary @file`. This
sidesteps every shell-quoting hazard the model output could trigger.

Cowork action: invoke the **Write tool** with `file_path` =
`/tmp/.tlinh-brainstorm-body.json` and `content` = the literal wrapper JSON:
```json
{"ideas": [
  {"title": "...", "angle": "...", "format": "...", "difficulty": "...", "viral_potential": 4},
  ... 4 more
]}
```
Then run:

```bash
<PREAMBLE>

# Per PLAN-v2 §7.4: non-lock endpoints retry exactly ONCE on 429 (or
# 5xx), then bubble up. Lock endpoints get 3 retries (not applicable here).
attempt=1
while [ $attempt -le 2 ]; do
  RESP=$(api PATCH "/api/articles/<CHOSEN_ID>/brainstorm" /tmp/.tlinh-brainstorm-body.json)
  STATUS=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f1)
  RETRY_AFTER=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f2)
  BODY=$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | cut -f3-)
  case "$STATUS" in
    200) echo "$BODY"; break ;;                                # {"id":...,"updated":true,"brainstormed_at":"..."}
    400) echo "[fatal] JSON validation: $BODY" >&2; exit 1 ;;  # fix the array shape and re-run
    429) if [ $attempt -lt 2 ]; then
           sleep "${RETRY_AFTER:-1}"
           attempt=$((attempt + 1))
           continue
         fi
         echo "[error] PATCH /brainstorm 429 after 1 retry" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    5*)  if [ $attempt -lt 2 ]; then
           echo "[transient] 5xx attempt $attempt; sleeping 2s" >&2
           sleep 2; attempt=$((attempt + 1)); continue
         fi
         echo "[error] PATCH /brainstorm 5xx after 1 retry: $BODY" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    *)   echo "[error] PATCH /brainstorm returned $STATUS: $BODY" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
done
```

The server allows re-brainstorming the same id (overwrites `ideas` + bumps
`brainstormed_at`); no CAS check.

### Step 7 — Display to user

Render the 5 ideas as a readable table:

```
✨ 5 ideas brainstormed cho article #42:

1. [Bài phân tích | Trung bình | Viral 4/5]
   "VinFast vs Tesla Q1: ai thực sự thắng thị trường VN?"
   Góc: So sánh data doanh số, không phải PR claim...

2. ...

Đã lưu vào VPS (brainstormed_at). Chọn idea nào để bắt đầu viết?
```

## Error handling

- **Article id not found** (Step 1/3 returns 404) → tell user; do NOT PATCH.
- **Content empty** in row → continue brainstorm using title only, mention in
  ideas that "cần verify content".
- **PATCH returns 400** (JSON validation failure on server) → review the
  generated JSON, fix shape, retry.
- **PATCH returns 5xx / network error** → retry once with 2s backoff, then
  surface the error to the user. Do NOT call /fail — brainstorm has no
  retry_count gating.
- **PATCH returns 429** → respect `Retry-After` header (typically 60s).

## Rules

- Always preserve `notified_at`, `scored_at`, `extracted_at` — `PATCH
  /brainstorm` only writes `ideas` + `brainstormed_at`, never touches other
  timestamps.
- Re-brainstorming the same id is allowed; it overwrites `ideas` with the
  latest run.
- Do NOT echo `$API_TOKEN`. The api() helper keeps it on stdin.
- Do NOT add steps not in this list. Stick to the 7 steps.
